WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.008 --> 00:00:04.750
We are now ready to define
profile Hidden Markov Models for

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.900
classifying proteins into families,
a difficult task in bioinformatics.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.570
Classifying proteins into families is
important because, as soon as a protein is

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:19.508
classified as a family member,
we know the function of this protein.

00:00:19.508 --> 00:00:24.480
However, the similarities may be
subtle for diverse proteins, and

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.660
therefore, pairwise
similarities may fail to detect

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.520
what family a given newly
sequenced protein belongs to.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:40.070
However, the protein may have weak
similarities with as many

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:45.750
proteins in the family, and it will help
us to assign this protein to a family.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.810
But we need new computational techniques
for finding such subtle similarities.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.710
Let's start from the alignment of some number
of proteins, as shown on this slide,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:05.470
and let's remove columns if the fraction
of insertions exceeds the threshold "theta",

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.930
the maximum fraction
of insertions per column.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.507
And after we've done this, we'll
construct an alignment where each

00:01:13.507 --> 00:01:17.111
column has a relatively small
number of insertions.

00:01:17.111 --> 00:01:20.802
We can further construct the
profile of this alignment.

00:01:20.802 --> 00:01:26.020
And finally, we will construct an HMM
corresponding to this profile.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.410
This is actually a strange HMM
because the probability of transition

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:36.910
from every state to the next state,
next to the right state is one.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:41.150
Therefore, there is nothing
probabilistic about this HMM with

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.220
respect to the transition probability matrix.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.480
However, with respect to emission
probabilities, there is a probabilistic

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.540
meaning of this HMM, and it corresponds to every

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.549
new protein having certain probabilities
of being emitted by this HMM.

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:09.550
But this HMM has a major flaw: it is not
clear how to model insertions in this HMM.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:16.000
For example, how would we represent this
protein in the framework of this HMM?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.810
Well, an obvious way is to add
insertion states, like this one,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.920
And if there are multiple insertions,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.970
we will also connect the
insertion state with itself.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.980
And finally,
we will design the HMM with
these insertion states.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:35.830
Now, we took care of insertions,
and this would be the path in

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:41.330
the HMM corresponding to this protein.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.830
But what about deletions?

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.700
And there is a simple way to account for
deletions,

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.990
let's say for this deletion,
by introducing transitions between

00:02:54.070 --> 00:03:00.100
all states to the right in this
HMM diagram, like this state.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.000
However, the question arises,
how many such edges

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.180
in the HMM diagram will we have after
adding all these possible transitions?

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:16.760
In this case, we added only transitions
for a given node of the HMM diagram,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.420
and there are already many edges.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:26.950
And as you remember, the running time of the
Viterbi algorithm equals the number of edges,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:32.540
or in the HMM diagram, multiplied by
the number of emitted symbols.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:38.670
So, solving the corresponding decoding
problem will be time consuming.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.375
However, there is a better way.

00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:44.850
We can introduce deletion
states in the HMM diagram.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.000
And in this case,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.270
we can route the sequence through these
newly-introduced deletion states.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.390
We need to introduce all of them,
of course,

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:58.990
and that's now how the deletion
issue will be addressed: with

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:04.270
introducing a relatively small number
of new edges in the HMM diagram.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.180
So here's the path corresponding to this

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.313
protein that is shown at
the bottom of this diagram.

00:04:13.313 --> 00:04:14.850
And I want to ask you a question:

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:18.340
Are there any edges missing
in this HMM diagram?

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.350
And of course, there are missing edges
between deletion and insertion states.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:27.470
We will add them, and
our HMM diagram is almost done.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:32.690
The only thing we need to add
is the start and end states.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.180
However, the question that
remains unanswered is:

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:44.750
What are the transition and
emission probabilities for this HMM?

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:50.400
And we wanted to define
the profile HMM Problem,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.030
which is to construct a profile
HMM from a multiple alignment.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:59.484
The input to this problem is a multiple
alignment and a threshold "theta",

00:04:59.484 --> 00:05:02.933
the maximum fraction of
insertions per column, and

00:05:02.933 --> 00:05:07.630
the output: transition and
emission matrices of the profile HMM.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:12.195
Let's construct hidden paths for

00:05:12.195 --> 00:05:16.226
every row of the multiple
alignment in the profile HMM.

00:05:16.226 --> 00:05:21.340
Please note that we are
constructing hidden paths

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.430
in the HMM diagram, not in
the Viterbi graph of the HMM.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.940
We haven't even constructed
the Viterbi graph yet.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:34.725
So for the top row
in the multiple alignment,

00:05:34.725 --> 00:05:38.805
this will be our green path
through the HMM diagram.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:42.505
This is the red path,
corresponding to the second row.

00:05:42.505 --> 00:05:46.295
And this is yet another path,
corresponding to the third row.

00:05:46.295 --> 00:05:54.195
And here all five paths correspond
to each row of the multiply alignment,

00:05:54.195 --> 00:05:56.900
and the question arises: How do we

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.620
compute transition probabilities
based on these paths?

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:08.090
Let's take a look at one particular
node in this HMM diagram and in these paths.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:14.080
Only four out of five paths
pass through this node,

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.500
and there are therefore four
transitions from this state, M5.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:24.710
Three of them go to the state I5, one goes

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:29.030
to the state M6, and zero go the state D6.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:35.563
And therefore, it makes sense to
define transition probabilities from

00:06:35.563 --> 00:06:41.520
M5 to I5 as three over four,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.700
transition probability from M5
to M6 as one over four, and

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:52.454
transition probability from
M5 to D6 as zero.

00:06:52.454 --> 00:06:59.137
It turns out that this common sense
assigning of transition probabilities

00:06:59.137 --> 00:07:04.880
actually corresponds to optimal assigning of
transition probabilities in the HMM diagram.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:06.910
But what about the emission probabilities?

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:11.650
In this case, we'll look at the node M2,

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:17.600
and it is traversed,
again, by four out of five paths,

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:23.070
and the emitted symbols are C,
F, C, and D from this state.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:27.830
And therefore, we define the emission
probabilities correspondingly

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:32.760
according to the count of
the emitted symbols from this state.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:38.160
We are almost done with
defining the profile HMM for

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.120
multiple alignment, but
let's consider a small complication

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:46.850
caused by forbidden transitions
in the HMM diagram.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:52.160
Please note that most transitions
between states in the profile

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.410
HMM are forbidden, and
the graph of the HMM diagram shown here

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:01.700
only shows the allowed transitions.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.720
So, the transition matrix,
matrix of transition probabilities, for

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.280
this HMM diagram, is mainly empty.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:13.120
We shown gray cells here corresponding
to allowed transitions, and

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.180
clear cells corresponding
to forbidden transitions.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.720
Everything is zero in
the forbidden transitions.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:23.970
But please note that there are also
many zeroes in the gray cells,

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.590
and you should worry about
these zeros because they simply

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:33.750
indicate that the number of rows in
our multiply alignment is small.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:37.330
And therefore,
don't forget about pseudocounts.

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.930
In this HMM, by adding
small pseudocounts for

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:46.610
every gray cell in the matrix of
transition probabilities for the profile HMM,

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:53.935
as we've usually done in the section when
we considered finding motifs in DNA.

